\id GAL Original Douay-Rheims (1582/1610)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Galatians
\toc1 The Epistle of S. Paul to the Galatians.
\toc2 Galatians
\toc3 GAL
\mt1 The Epistle of S. Paul to the Galatians.
\is THE ARGUMENT OF THE EPISTLE OF S. PAUL TO THE GALATIANS.
\ip THAT this Epiſtle may ſeeme to be the firſt that S. Paul wrote, was declared in the Argument of the Epiſtle to the Romanes, notwithſtanding that in the ſecond chapter it is euident to haue bene written 14 yeres at the leaſt after his Conuerſion, and (as it is ſaid) from Epheſus, belike at that time of his being there, which is mentioned Act. 18.
\ip The occaſion of it were ſuch Falſe apoſtles, as we reade of, Act. 15. Et quidam deſcendentes, &c. \it And certaine comming downe from Iewrie, taught the brethren\it* (that is the Chriſtian Gentiles at Antioche) \it that vnles you be circumciſed according to the manner of Moyſes, you cannot be ſaued.\it* Such commers alſo to the Galatians (whom S. Paul had conuerted Act. 16, as himſelf mentioneth Gal. 1. and 4.) did ſeduce them, ſaying, that al the other Apoſtles to whom they ſhould rather harken then to Paul (who came they knew not from whence) did vſe Circumciſion: yea and that Paul himſelf, when he came among them, durſt do none other. And to winne them more eaſily, they did not lay on them the burden of the whole Law, but of Circumciſion only.
\ip Againſt theſe deceiuers, S. Paul declareth, that he receiued his Apoſtleſhip and learned the Goſpel that he preacheth, of Chriſt himſelf after his Reſurrection: and that the other Apoſtles (although he learned nothing of them) receiued him into their ſocietie, and allowed wel of his preaching to the Gentiles, though themſelues being Jews, and liuing among the Jews, had not yet left the ceremonies of the Law: howbeit they did not put in them any hope of iuſtification, but in Chriſt alone without them. He declareth moreouer, that the ſaid Falſe apoſtles belyed him, in ſaying that he alſo preached Circumciſion ſometimes. Againe, that they themſelues in preaching no more but Circumciſion, did againſt the nature of Circumciſion, becauſe it is a profeſſion to obſerue the whole Law: finally, whatſoeuer they pretended, that indeed they did it onely to pleaſe the Jews, of whom otherwiſe they ſhould be perſecuted.
\ip So that in this Epiſtle he handleth the ſame matter, which in the Epiſtle to the Romanes: but here leſſe exactly and more briefly, becauſe the Galatians were very rude, and the Romanes contrariwiſe, repleti omni ſcientia (Rom. 15) \it repleniſhed with al knowledge.\it*
\c 1
\cd After the foundation laide in the ſalutation, 6 he exclaimeth againſt the Galatians, and their falſe apoſtles, 11 conſidering that the Goſpel which he preached to them, he had it immediatly of Chriſt him ſelf. 13 Which to ſhew he beginneth to tel the ſtorie of his conuerſion and preaching ſince then, that as he learned nothing of the other Apoſtles, ſo yet he had their approbation.
\p
\v 1 \sc PAVL\sc* an Apoſtle not of men, neither by man, but by \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt, and God the Father that raiſed him from the dead, \ef - \fr 1.1 \fq Neither by man. \ft ¹ Though he vvere not firſt by mans election, nomination, or aſſignement, but by Gods owne ſpecial appointmét, choſen to be an Apoſtle: yet by the like expreſſe ordinance of God he tooke orders or impoſition of hands of men, as is plaine Act. 13. Let vs bevvare then of ſuch falſe Apoſtles, as now a daies intrude them ſelues to the office of Miniſterie and preaching, neither called of God, nor rightly ordered of men. \fq ¹ \ft S. Paul sent to preache by ordinarie imposition of handes.\ef*
\v 2 and al the brethren that are vvith me: to the churches of Galatia.
\v 3 Grace to you and peace from God the Father and our Lord \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt,
\v 4 vvho gaue him ſelf for our ſinnes, that he might deliuer vs from this preſent vvicked vvorld, according to the vvil of our God and father:
\v 5 to vvhom is glorie for euer and euer. Amen.
\v 6 I maruel that thus ſo ſoone you are transferred from him that called you into the grace of Chriſt, vnto an other Goſpel:
\v 7 which is not an other, vnles there be ſome that trouble you, and vvil \f 1 \fr 1.7 \ft New Goſpellers that peruert, corrupt, or alter the one onely true and firſt deliuered Goſpel, are to be auoided. See S. Auguſtine \+it Cont. Fauſtum li. 32. c. 27\+it*.\f* inuert the Goſpel of Chriſt.
\v 8 But although we, or an Angel from heauen, euangelize to you beſide that vvhich vve haue euangelized to you, be he anáthema. \ef - \fr 1.8 \fq Or an Angel. \ft ¹ Many worthie obſeruations are made in the fathers writings, of the earneſt admonition of the Apoſtle, and much may we gather of the text it ſelf, firſt, that the credit of any man or Angel for what learning, eloquence, ſhevv of grace or vertue ſo euer, though he vvrought miracles, ſhould not moue a Chriſtian man from that truth vvhich he hath once receiued in the Catholike Church: of which point Vincentius Lirinenſis excellently treateth. \+it li. cont. profan. hæres. Nouitates\+it*. Vvhereby vve may ſee that it is great pitie and ſhame, that ſo many folow Luther and Caluin and ſuch other leude fellovves, into a nevv Goſpel, vvhich are ſo farre from Apoſtles and Angels, that they are not any vvhit comparable vvith the old Heretikes in giftes of learning or eloquence, much leſſe in good life. ² Secondly S. Auguſtine ³ noteth vpon the word, \+it Beſide\+it*, that not al other teaching, or more preaching then the firſt, is forbidden, but ſuch as is contrarie and diſagreing to the rule of faith. \+it The Apoſtle did not ſay\+it*, ſaith he, \+it If any man euangelize to you more then you haue receiued, but, beſide that you receiued. for if he ſhould ſay that, he ſhould be preiudicial to him ſelf, who coueted to come to the Theſſalonians, that he might ſupply that vvhich was vvanting to their faith. Now he that ſupplieth, addeth that which vvas lacking, taketh not avvay that which vvas, &c\+it*. By which we ſee how friuolouſly and calumniouſly the Heretikes charge the Church vvith addition to the Scriptures. ⁴ Thirdly, as vvel by the vvord \+it euangelizamus\+it* (vve euangelize) as the word \+it accepiſtis\+it* (you haue receiued) vve may note that the firſt truth, againſt which no ſecond Goſpelling or doctrine may be admitted, is not that onely which he wrote to the Galatians, or vvhich is conteined either in his or any other of the Apoſtles or Euangeliſtes vvritings, but that which was by word of mouth alſo preached, taught, or deliuered them firſt, before he wrote to them. Therfore the Aduerſaries of the Church that meaſure the word of God or Goſpel by the Scriptures onely, thinking them ſelues not to incurre S. Paules curſe, except they teach directly againſt the written word, are fouly beguiled. As therein alſo they ſhamfully erre, when they charge the Catholikes with adding to the Goſpel, when they teach any thing that is not in expreſſe wordes written by the Apoſtles or Euangeliſtes. not marking that the Apoſtle in this Chapter, and els where, commonly calleth his & his fellovves whole preaching, the Goſpel, be it written or vnwritten. ⁵ Fourthly, by the ſame wordes we ſee condemned al after-preachings, later doctrines, new ſectes and authors of the ſame: that onely being true, which was firſt by the Apoſtles and Apoſtolike men as the lavvful huſbandmen of Chriſtes fild, ſovved and planted in the Church: and that falſe, which was later and as it vvere ouerſovven by the enemie. By which rule not onely Tertulian \+it (de præſcript. nu. 6 & 9.) but all other auncient Doctors, and ſpecially S. Irenæus\+it* (li. 3. c. 2. 3. 4.) tried truth from falſehod, and condemned old Heretikes, prouing Marcion, Valentine, Cerdon, Menander, and ſuch like, falſe Apoſtles, becauſe they came in with their nouelties long after the Church was ſettled in former truth. ⁶ Sixthly, This curſe or execration pronounced by the Apoſtle, toucheth not onely the Galatians, or thoſe of the Apoſtles time, that preached othervviſe then they did, but it perteineth to al times, preachers, and teachers, vnto the worldes end, and it concerneth them (as Vincentius Lirinenſis ſaith) that preach a new faith, or change that old faith which they receiued in the vnitie of the Catholike Church. \+it To preach any thing to Chriſtian Catholike men\+it* (ſaith he) ⁷ \+it beſides that which they haue receiued, neuer was it lawful, neuer is it, nor neuer ſhal it be lawful. to ſay anáthema to ſuch it hath been, and is, and ſhal be alvvaies behooful\+it*. So S. Auguſtine by this place holdeth al accurſed, that draw a Chriſtian man from the ſocietie of the whole Church, to make the ſeueral part of any one ſect: that call to the hidden conuenticles of Heretikes, from the open and knowen Church of Chriſt: that allure to the priuate, from the common: finally al that draw with chatting curioſitie the children of the Catholike Church, by teaching any thing beſides that they found in the church. \+it ep. 48. Pſal. 103. Con. 2\+it*. ⁸ mentioning alſo that a Donatiſt feined an Angel to haue admoniſhed him to call his frende out of the Communion of the Catholike Church into his ſect. and he ſaith, that if it had been an Angel in deede, yet ſhould he not haue heard him. Laſtly S. Hierom vſeth this place, wherein the Apoſtle giueth the curſe or anáthema to al falſe teachers not once but tvviſe, to proue that the zeale ⁹ of Catholike men ought to be ſo great toward al Heretikes and their doctrines, that they ſhould giue them the anáthema, though they vvere neuer ſo deere vnto them. In which caſe, ſaith this holy Doctor, I would not ſpare mine ovvne parents. \+it Ad Pammach. c. 3. cont. Io. Hieros\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft No shew of learning or vertue must moue vs from the faith. \fq ² \ft Li. cont. proph. hær. nouit. \fq ³ \ft \+it Tract. 98. in Ioan.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft The Gospel is not onely in the Written word of scripture, but in vnwritten tradition also. \fq ⁵ \ft After-preaching & ouersowing of nouelties, argueth false doctrine. \fq ⁶ \ft The Apostles curse vpon al that teache new doctrine, and draw men from the Cath. Church. \fq ⁷ \ft \+it Preaching cótrarie to the faith receiued is forbidden, not other preaching\+it*. \fq ⁸ \ft * \+it Aug. ep. 165\+it*. \fq ⁹ \ft Zeale against heretikes.\ef*
\v 9 As we haue ſaid before, ſo novv I ſay againe, If any euangelize to you, beſide that vvhich you haue receiued, be he anáthema.
\v 10 For do I novv vſe perſuaſion to men, or to God? Or do I ſeeke to pleaſe men? If I yet did pleaſe men, I ſhould not be the ſeruant of Chriſt.
\v 11 \f 1 \fr 1.11 \ft The Epiſtle vpon the Commemoration of S. Paul, \+it Iun. 30\+it*.\f* For I doe you to vnderſtand, brethren, the Goſpel that vvas euangelized of me, that it is not according to man.
\v 12 For neither did I receiue it of man, nor learne it: but by the reuelation of \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt.
\v 13 For you haue heard my conuerſation ſometime in Iudaiſme, that aboue meaſure I perſecuted the Church of God, and expugned it, \x - \xt Act. 9, 1.\x*
\v 14 and profited in Iudaiſme aboue many of mine equales in my nation, being more aboundantly an emulator of the traditions of my fathers.
\v 15 But vvhen it pleaſed him that ſeparated me from my mothers vvombe, and called me by his grace, to reueale his ſonne in me,
\v 16 that I ſhould euangelize him among the Gentils, incontinent I condeſcended not to fleſh and bloud,
\v 17 neither came I to Hieruſalem to the Apoſtles my anteceſſors: but I vvent into Arabia, and againe I returned to Damaſcus.
\v 18 Then, after three yeres I came to Hieruſalem to ſee Peter: and taried with him fiftene daies. \ef - \fr 1.18 \fq To ſee Peter. \ft ¹ In what eſtimation S. Peter was with this Apoſtle, it appeareth: ſeing for reſpect and honour of his perſon, and of duety as Tertullian \+it de præſcript\+it*. ſaith (notwithſtanding his great affaires Eccleſiaſticall) he vvent ſo farre to ſee him. not in vulgar maner, but (as S. Chryſoſtom noteth the Greeke word ² to import, ἱστορῆσαι) to behold him as men behold a thing or perſon of name, excellencie, and maieſtie. for vvhich cauſe, and to fill him ſelf with the perfect vew of his behauiour, he abode with him fiftene daies. See S. Hierom \+it ep. 103 ad Paulinum. to. 3\+it*. who maketh alſo a myſterie of the number of daies that he taried with S. Peter. See S. Ambroſe \+it in Comment. huius loci\+it*, and S. Chryſoſtome vpon this place, and \+it ho. 87 in Ioan\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft S. Paul doth visite S. Peter of honour and reuerence toward him. \fq ² \ft \+it ἱστορῆσαι.\+it*\ef*
\v 19 But other of the Apoſtles ſavv I none: ſauing Iames \f 1 \fr 1.19 \ft S. Iames was called our Lordes brother after the hebrew phraſe of the Iewes, by which neere kinſmen are called brethren, for they were not brethren in deede, but rather ſiſters children.\f* the brother of our Lord.
\v 20 And the things that I vvrite to you: behold before God, that I lie not. ⊢
\v 21 After that, I came into the partes of Syria and Cilicia.
\v 22 And I vvas vnknowen by ſight to the churches of Ievvrie, that vvere in Chriſt:
\v 23 but they had heard only, That he vvhich perſecuted vs ſometime, doth novv euangelize the faith vvhich ſometime he expugned:
\v 24 and in me they glorified God.
\c 2
\cd He telleth furth the ſtorie begonne in the laſt chapter, and how he reprehended Peter, 15 and then ſpecially vrgeth the enſample of the Chriſtian Iewes, who ſought vnto Chriſt for iuſtification, and that by warrant alſo of their Law it ſelf, as alſo becauſe othervviſe Chriſts death had been needles.
\p
\v 1 \sc THEN\sc* after fourtene yeres I vvent vp againe to Hieruſalem vvith Barnabas, taking Titus alſo vvith me.
\v 2 And I vvent vp according to reuelation: and cóferred with them the Goſpel which I preach among the Gentiles, but apart with them that ſeemed to be ſomething, leſt perhaps in vaine I ſhould runne or had runne. \ef - \fr 2.2 \fq Conferred with them. \ft ¹ Though S. Paul vvere taught his Goſpel of God and not of man, and had an extraordinarie calling by Chriſt him ſelf, yet by reuelation he vvas ſent to Hieruſalem to conferre the ſaid Goſpel which he preached, vvith his elders the ordinarie Apoſtles and Rulers of the Church, to put both his vocation and doctrine to their trial and approbation, and to ioyne in office, teaching, and ſocietie or communion with them. For there is no extraordinarie or miraculous vocation, that can ſeuer or ſeparate the perſon ſo called, in doctrine or fellovvſhip of Chriſtian life and religion, from the ordinarie knowen ſocietie of Gods people and Prieſtes. ² Therfore vvhoſoeuer he be (vpon what pretence ſo euer) that vvil not haue his calling and doctrine tried by the ordinarie Gouerners of Gods Church, or diſdaineth to go vp to the principal place of our religion, to conferre vvith Peter and other pillers of the Church, it is euident that he is a falſe teacher, a Schiſmatike, and an Heretike. By vvhich rule you may trie al your nevv teachers of Luthers or Caluins ſchoole: vvho neuer did nor euer durſt put their preaching to ſuch conference or trial of holy Councel or Biſhops, as they ought to do, and vvould do, if it vvere of God, as S. Paules vvas. \fq ¹ \ft S. Paul conferreth with S. Peter and the rest, for trial of his doctrine. \fq ² \ft The heretikes submit their doctrine to no trial of Bishops or Coúcel.\ef* \ef - \fr 2.2 \fq In vaine. \ft ¹ Though S. Paul doubted not of the truth of the Goſpel which he preached, knovving it to be of the holy Ghoſt: yet becauſe other men could not, nor would not acknowledge ſo much, til it vvere allovved by ſuch as vvere vvithout al exception knowen to be Apoſtles & to haue the ſpirit of truth, to diſcerne whether the vocation, ſpirit, & Goſpel of Paul vvere of God, he knevv he ſhould othervviſe vvithout conferéce vvith them, haue loſt his labour, both for the time paſt and to come. \+it He had not had\+it* (ſaith S. Hierom) \+it ſecuritie of preaching the Goſpel, if it had not been approued by Peters ſentence and the reſt that vvere vvith him. Hiero ep. 89. c. 2. See Tertul. li. 4 Cont. Marc. nu. 3\+it*. Therfore by reuelation he went to conferre with the Apoſtles at Hieruſalem, that by them hauing his Apoſtleſhip and Goſpel liked and approued, he might preach with more fruite. Wherein vve ſee, this holy Apoſtle did not as the ſeditious proud Heretikes do now a daies, vvhich refuſing al mans atteſtation or approbation, vvil be tried by Scriptures onely. ² As alſo vve may learne that it is no ſuch abſurditie as the Aduerſaries vvould make it, to haue the Scriptures approued by the Churches teſtimonie. Seing the Goſpel vvhich S. Paul preached (being of as much certaintie and of the ſame Holy Ghoſt that the Scriptures be) was to be put in conference and examination of the Apoſtles, without al derogation to the truth, dignitie, or certaintie of the ſame. And the cauilling of Heretikes, that we make ſubiect Gods Oracles to mans cenſure, and the Scriptures to haue no more force then the Church is content to graunt vnto them, is vaine and falſe. ³ For, to beare vvitnes or to giue euidence or atteſtation that the preaching or writing of ſuch, is true and of the Holy Ghoſt, is not to make it true: no more then the Goldſmith or touch-ſtone that trie and diſcerne which is true gold, make it good gold, but they giue euidence to man that ſo it is. And therfore that diſputation alſo, vvhether the Scripture or the Church be of greater authoritie, is ſuperfluous: either giuing teſtimonie to the other, and both aſſured by the Holy Ghoſt from al error: ⁴ the Church yet being before the Scriptures, the ſpouſe of Chriſt, and proper dwelling, temple, or ſubiect of God and his graces: for the which Church the Scriptures vvere, and not the Church for the Scriptures. In which Church there is iudicial authoritie by office and iuriſdiction to determine of doubtful queſtions touching the ſenſe of the Scriptures and other controuerſies in religion, & to puniſh diſobedient perſons. Of which iudicial power the Scriptures be not capable, as neither the truthes & determinations of the ſame can be ſo euident to men, nor ſo agreable and fit for euery particular reſolution, as diuerſitie of times and perſons requireth. Certaine is the truth, and great is the authoritie of both: but in ſuch diuers kindes, as they can not be vvell compared together. The controuerſie is much like as if a man touching the ruling a caſe in lavv or giuing ſentence in a matter of queſtion, ſhould aſke, whether the iudge, or the euidéce of the parties, be of more authoritie or credit, vvhich were as friuolous a diſpute, as it were a diſordered part for any man to ſay, he vvould be tried by no other iudge but by his owne writings or euidences. With ſuch triflers and ſeditious perſons haue we to do now a daies in diuinitie, as were intolerable in any prophane ſcience or facultie in the vvorld. \fq ¹ \ft The approbation of S. Paules doctrine by Peter & the rest, was very requisite. \fq ² \ft No absurditie that the Scriptures be approued by the Churches testimonie. \fq ³ \ft The Church maketh not Canonical Scripture, but declareth that it is so. \fq ⁴ \ft The Scripture & Church cópared together for antiquitie, authoritie, &c.\ef*
\v 3 But neither Titus which vvas with me, vvhereas he vvas a Gentil, vvas compelled to be circumciſed:
\v 4 but becauſe of the falſe brethren craftely brought in, vvhich craftely came in to eſpie our libertie that vve haue in Chriſt \sc IESVS\sc*, that they might bring vs into ſeruitude.
\v 5 To vvhom vve yelded not ſubiection no not for an houre, that the truth of the Goſpel may remaine vvith you.
\v 6 But of them that ſeemed to be ſomething, (vvhat they vvere ſometime, it is nothing to me. \x - \xt Deu. 10, 17.\x* God accepteth not the perſon of man) for to me, they that ſeemed to be ſomething, added nothing. \ef - \fr 2.6 \fq Added nothing. \ft ¹ The Goſpel and preaching of S. Paul vvas wholy of God, & therfore though it vvere put to the Churches probation, as gold is to the touch ſtone: yet being found in al pointes pure, nothing could be altered or amended therein by the Apoſtles. Euen ſo the Scriptures which are in deede vvholy of the Holy Ghoſts enditing, being put to the Churches trial, are found, proued, and teſtified vnto the vvorld to be ſuch, and not made true, altered, or amended by the ſame. Vvithout which atteſtation of the Church, the holy Scriptures in them ſelues vvere alvvaies true before: but not ſo knowen to be, to al Chriſtians, nor they ſo bound to take them. And that is the meaning of the famous ſentence of S. Auguſtine \+it Cont. ep. fund. c. 5\+it*. vvhich troubleth the Heretikes ſo much. \+it I would not beleeue the Goſpel\+it* (ſaith he) \+it vnles the authoritie of the Church moued me\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft The Scriptures alwaies true in them selues, are so knowen to be by the Church.\ef*
\v 7 But contrarievviſe vvhen they had ſeen, that to me vvas committed the Goſpel of the \f 1 \fr 2.7 \ft See the marginal Annotation \+it Rom. 2. v. 25\+it*.\f* prepuce, as to Peter of the circumciſion ( \ef - \fr 2.7 \fq To Peter of the circumciſion. \ft ¹ We may not thinke, as the Heretikes deceitfuly teach, that the charge of the Apoſtles vvas ſo diſtincted, that none could preach or exerciſe iuriſdiction but in thoſe ſeueral places or towardes thoſe peoples or prouinces onely, wherevnto by Gods appointment or their owne lot or election, they vvere ſpecially deſigned. For, euery Apoſtle might by Chriſtes commiſsion \+it (Mat. 28. Goe, and teach al nations\+it*) vſe al ſpiritual functió through the whole vvorld, yet for the more particular regard & care of prouinces, & for peace & order ſake, ſome were appointed to one countrie, & ſome to an other: as, of the other Apoſtles, we ſee in the Eccleſiaſtical hiſtories, and for S. Peter & S. Paul, it is plaine by this place & other, ² that to them as to the tvvo cheefe and moſt renowmed Apoſtles, the Church of al Nations vvas giuen, as deuided into two partes, that is, Ievves and Gentiles: the firſt and principal being S. Peters lot, that herein alſo he might reſemble our Sauiour, vvho was ſent namely ³ \+it to the loſt ſheepe of Iſrael\+it* (Mat. 15.), and vvas properly ⁴ \+it the Miniſter of the Circumciſion\+it* (Ro. 15.): the ſecond being S. Paules, whom Chriſt choſe ſpecially to preach to the Gentiles: Not ſo for al that, that either he vvas limited to the Gentils onely, (whó the Actes of the Apoſtles report, in euery place, firſt to haue entered into the Synagogs & preached Chriſt to the Iewes, as he wrote alſo to the Hebrues & euer had ſpecial regard & honour to thé:) or Peter ſo boúd to the Ievves only, that he could not meddle vvith the Gentiles: ſeing he vvas ⁵ the man choſen of God, by whom the Gentils ſhould firſt beleeue (Act. 10. & 15. v. 7.), vvho firſt baptized them, and firſt gaue order concerning them. Therfore the treacherie of Caluin (Calu. li. 4. c. 6. nu. 15. Inſtit.) ⁶ ⁷ is intolerable, that vpon this diſtinction of the Apoſtles charge, would haue the ſimple ſuppoſe, that S. Peter could not be Biſhop of Rome (ſo might he barre S. Iohn from Epheſus alſo) nor deale among the Gentiles, as a thing againſt Gods ordinance and the appointment betwene him and S. Paul: as though thereby the one had bound him ſelf to the other, not to preach or meddle vvithin his fellovves compaſſe. And vvhich is further moſt ſeditious, he exhorteth al men to keepe faſt the foreſaid compact, and rather to haue reſpect to S. Paules Apoſtleſhip, then to S. Peters: as though the preaching, authoritie, and Apoſtleſhip of both vvere not a like true, and al of one holy Spirit, whether they preached to Iewes or Gentiles, as both did preach vnto both peoples, as is already proued, and at length, partly by the daily decay of the Iewiſh ſtate and their incredulitie, and partly for that in Chriſtianitie the diſtinction of Iew & Gentil ceaſed after a ſeaſon: ⁸ both vvent to the cheefe citie of the Gentiles, and there founded the Church common to the Hebrues and al nations, Peter firſt, and Paul afterward. And therfore Tertullian ſaith, \+it de præſcript. nu. 14. O happie Church, to which the Apoſtles powred out al doctrine with their bloud. Where Peter ſuffereth like to our Lordes paſſion, where Paul is crowned vvith Iohn (Baptiſts) death\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft The Apostles commissió general through the world, & yet peculiar to certaine prouinces. \fq ² \ft Neither Peter only of the Iewes, nor Paul Apostle of the Gétiles only. \fq ³ \ft \+it Mat. 15.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Ro. 15.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Act. 10. & 15. v. 7.\+it* \fq ⁶ \ft Caluins folish reason that Peter was not B. of Rome, & his derogació from Peters Apostleship. \fq ⁷ \ft \+it Calv. li. 4. c. 6. nu. 15. Instit.\+it* \fq ⁸ \ft The Church founded at Rome by S. Peter and S. Paul.\ef*
\v 8 for he that vvrought in Peter to the Apoſtleſhip of the circumciſion, vvrought in me alſo among the Gentils)
\v 9 and vvhen they had knovven the grace that vvas giuen me, Iames and Cephas and Iohn, vvhich ſeemed to be pillers, gaue to me and Barnabas the right handes of ſocietie: that vve vnto the Gentiles, & they vnto the circumciſion: \ef - \fr 2.9 \fq Gaue the right handes of ſocietie. \ft ¹ There is and alwaies ought to be, a common fellowſhip and fraternitie of al Paſtors and preachers of the Church. Into which ſocietie who ſo euer entereth not, but ſtandeth in Schiſme and ſeparation from Peter and the cheefe Apoſtolike Paſtors, what pretence ſo euer he hath, or vvhence ſo euer he chalengeth authoritie, he is a wolfe, and no true Paſtor. Which vnion and communion together was ſo neceſſarie euen in S. Paules caſe, that, notvvithſtanding his ſpecial calling of God, yet the Holy Ghoſt cauſed him to go vp to his elder Apoſtles, to be receiued into their fellowſhip or brotherhod. for it is to be noted, that \+sc SS\+sc*. Peter, Iames, and Iohn were not ſent to S. Paul, to ioyne vvith him or to be tried for their doctrine and calling, by him: but contrariewiſe he vvas ſent to them as to the cheefe and knowen ordinarie Apoſtles. They therfore gaue Paul their handes, that is to ſay, tooke him into their ſocietie, and not he them. And S. Hieroms rule concerning this, ſhal be found true to the vvorldes end, ſpeaking of S. Peters ſucceſſor: \+it He that gathereth not with thee, ſcattereth. Ep. 57\+it*. And in an other place for the ſame cauſe he calleth Rome, \+it tutiſſimum Communionis portum\+it*, the moſt ſafe and ſure hauen of communion or Societie. \+it Ep. 16. c. 3\+it*. ² And vvhereas the Heretikes by this alſo would proue that Peter had no preeminéce aboue Paul being his fellow Apoſtle, it is ridiculous. As though al of one fellowſhip or brotherhod be alvvaies equal, or as though there were not order and gouernement, ſuperioritie and inferioritie, in euery ſocietie wel appointed. And they might perceiue by this vvhole paſſage, that Peter vvas the ſpecial and in more ſingular ſort, the Apoſtle of the Iewes, though Iames and Iohn were alſo: as S. Paul is alſo called in more ſingular ſort the Apoſtle and doctor of the Gentiles then S. Barnabas, and yet they vvere both alike taken here into this ſocietie, as they were both at once and alike ſegregated into this miniſterie, and ordered together \+it Act. 13\+it*. It is a poore reaſon then to ſay or thinke, S. Peter not to be aboue S. Barnabas neither, becauſe of this ſocietie and fellowſhip vnto which he vvas receiued together vvith S. Paul. \fq ¹ \ft Al Catholike preachers and Pastors must communicate with Peter & his successors. \fq ² \ft The heretikes ridiculous argumét against Peters preeminence.\ef*
\v 10 only that vve ſhould be mindeful of the poore: the vvhich ſame thing alſo I vvas careful to doe.
\v 11 And vvhen Cephas vvas come to Antioche, I reſiſted him \f 1 \fr 2.11 \ft That is, \+it in preſence, before them al\+it*, as Beza him ſelf expoundeth it. Yet the Engliſh Bezites to the more diſgracing of S. Peter, tranſlate, \+it to his face\+it*, No. Teſt. an. 1580.\f*\f 2 \fr 2.11 \ft \+it κατὰ πρόσωπον\+it*\f* in face, becauſe he vvas reprehenſible. \ef - \fr 2.11 \fq I reſiſted him. \ft ¹ Vvicked Porphyrie (as S. Hierom vvriteth) chargeth S. Paul of enuie and malepart boldnes, and S. Peter of errour. \+it Proæm. Comment. in Galat\+it*. Euen ſo the like impious ſonnes of Cham, for this, and for other things, gladly charge S. Peter, as though he had committed the greateſt crimes in the world. for, it is the propertie of Heretikes and il men, to be glad to ſee the Saincts reprehended and their faultes diſcouered, as vve may learne in the writings of S. Auguſtine againſt Fauſtus the Manichee, who gathered out al the actes of the holy Patriarches, that might ſeeme to the people to be vvorthy blame. ² Vvhom the ſaid holy Doctor defendeth at large againſt him, as both he, and before him S. Cyprian, finde here vpon this Apoſtles reprehenſion, much matter of praiſing both their vertues: S. Paules great zeale, and S. Peters vvonderful humilitie: that the one in the cauſe of God vvould not ſpare his Superior, and that the other, in that excellent dignitie, would not take it in il part, nor by allegation of his Supremacie diſdaine or refuſe to be controwled by his iunior, vvhich of the two they count the greater grace and more to be imitated. \+it For neither Peter\+it* (ſaith S. Cyprian) \+it whom our Lord choſe the firſt and vpon whom he built the Church, when Paul diſputed with him of circumciſion, chalenged inſolently or arrogantly tooke any thing to him ſelf, ſaying that he had the Primacie, and therfore the later Diſciples ought rather to obey him\+it*. ep. 71 ad Quintum. nu. 2. And S. Auguſtine ep. 19. c. 2 in fine. \+it That\+it* (ſaith he) \+it which vvas done of Paul profitably by the libertie of charitie, the ſame Peter tooke in good part by holy and benigne godlines of humilitie, and ſo he gaue vnto poſteritie a more rare and holy example, if at any time perhaps they did amis, to be content to be corrected of their iuniors, then Paul, for to be bold and confident: yea the inferiors to reſiſt their betters for defending the truth of the Goſpel, brotherly charitie alwaies preſerued\+it*. ³ By which notable ſpeaches of the Doctors vve may alſo ſee, how friuolouſly the Heretikes argue herevpon, that S. Peter could not be Superior to S. Paul, being ſo reprehended of him: whereas the Fathers make it an example to the Superiors, to beare with humilitie the correption or controulement euen of their inferiors. Namely by this example S. Auguſtine \+it (li. 2 de bapt. c. 1\+it*.) excellently declareth, that the B. Martyr S. Cyprian, vvho walked avvry touching the rebaptizing of them that were chriſtened of Heretikes, could not, nor vvould not haue been offended to be admoniſhed & reformed in that point by his fellovves or inferiors, much leſſe by a whole Councel. \+it We haue learned\+it*, ſaith he, \+it that Peter the Apoſtle, in whom the Primacie of the Apoſtles by excellent grace is ſo preeminent, when he did othervviſe concerning circumciſion then the truth required, was corrected of Paul the later Apoſtle. I thinke (without any reproch vnto him) Cyprian the Biſhop may be compared to Peter the Apoſtle. howbeit I ought rather to feare leſt I be iniurious to Peter. for who knoweth not that the principalitie of Apoſtleſhip it to be preferred before any dignitie of Biſhop whatſoeuer? but if the grace of the Chaires or Sees differ, yet the glorie of the Martyrs is one\+it*. ⁴ And who is ſo dull that can not ſee, that the inferior, though not by office and iuriſdiction, yet by the law of brotherly loue and fraternal correption, may reprehend his ſuperior? Did euer any man wonder that a good Prieſt or any vertuous perſon ſhould tell the Pope, or any other great Prelate, or greateſt Prince in earth, their faultes? Popes may be reprehended, and are iuſtly admoniſhed of their faultes, and ought to take it in good part, & ſo they do and euer haue done, vvhen it commeth of zeale & loue, as of S. Paul, Irenæus, Cyprian, Hierom, Auguſtine, Bernard: ⁵ but of Simon Magus, Nouatus, Iulian, Wicleffe, Luther, Caluin, Beza, that do it of malice, & raile no leſſe at their vertues then their vices, of ſuch (I ſay) Gods Prelates muſt not be taught nor corrected, though they muſt patiently take it, as our Sauiour did the like reproches of the malitious Iewes, and as Dauid did the malediction of Semei. \+it 2 Reg. 16\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft The heretikes malitiously derogate from S. Peter. \fq ² \ft Paules reprehension of Peter, teacheth vs the zeale of the one, and humilitie of the other. \fq ³ \ft It proueth nothing agaist Peters superioritie, that he was reprehended. \fq ⁴ \ft The superior may be reprehended or admonished of the inferior. \fq ⁵ \ft Heretikes reprehension of Catholike Bishops is rather railing.\ef* \ef - \fr 2.11 \fq Reprehenſible. \ft ¹ The Heretikes hereof againe inferre, that Peter then did erre in faith, and therfore the Popes may faile therein alſo. To which vve anſwer, that hovvſoeuer other Popes may erre in their priuate teachings or writings, whereof vve haue treated before in the Annotation vpon theſe vvordes, ² \+it That thy faith faile not\+it*: it is certaine that S. Peter did not here faile in faith, nor erre in doctrine or knowledge. for it vvas \+it conuerſationis non prædicationis vitium\+it*, as Tertullian ſaith. \+it de præſcript. nu. 7\+it*. It vvas a default in conuerſation, life, or regiment, which may be committed of any man, be he neuer ſo holy, and not in doctrine. S. Auguſtine and vvhoſoeuer make moſt of it, thinke no othervviſe of it. But S. Hierom and ³ many other holy fathers deeme it to haue been no fault at all, nor any other thing then S. Paul him ſelf did vpon the like occaſion: & that this whole combat was a ſet thing agreed vpon betwene them. It is a ſchoole point much debated betwixt S. Hierom and S. Auguſtine \+it ep. 9. 11. 19. apud Auguſtin\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft S. Peters errour was not in faith, but in conuersation or behauiour. \fq ² \ft \+it Luc. 22, 32\+it*. \fq ³ \ft \+it * See S. Chrysost. Theophyl. &c.\+it*\ef*
\v 12 For before that certaine came from Iames, he did eate with the Gentiles: but vvhen they vvere come, he vvithdrevv and ſeparated him ſelf, fearing them that vvere of the circumciſion.
\v 13 And to his ſimulation conſented the reſt of the Ievves, ſo that Barnabas alſo vvas ledde of them into that ſimulation.
\v 14 But vvhen I ſavv that they vvalked not rightly to the veritie of the Goſpel, I ſaid to Cephas before them al: If thou being a Ievve, liueſt Gentile-like & not Iudaically: hovv doeſt thou compel the Gentils to Iudaize.
\v 15 We are by nature Ievves, and not of the Gentils, ſinners.
\v 16 But knovving that \x - \xt Ro. 3, 19. 20.\x* man is not iuſtified by the vvorkes \f 2 \fr 2.16 \ft Ro. 3, 19. 20. · By this and by the diſcourſe of this whole epiſtle, you may perceiue, that when iuſtification is attributed to faith, the vvorkes of Charitie, be not excluded, but the workes of Moyſes law: that is, the ceremonies, ſacrifices and ſacraments thereof principally, and conſequently al workes done merely by nature and free wil, without the faith, grace, ſpirit, & aide of Chriſt.\f* of the Lavv, but by the faith of \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt: we alſo beleeue in Chriſt \sc IESVS\sc*, that we may be iuſtified by the faith of Chriſt, and not by the vvorkes of the Lavv: for the vvhich cauſe, by the workes of the Law no fleſh ſhal be iuſtified.
\v 17 But if ſeeking to be iuſtified in Chriſt, our ſelues alſo be found ſinners: is Chriſt then a miniſter of ſinne? God forbid.
\v 18 For if I build the ſame things againe vvhich I haue deſtroied, I make my ſelf a preuaricatour.
\v 19 For I by the Lavv, am dead to the Law, that I may liue to God: vvith Chriſt I am nailed to the croſſe.
\v 20 And I liue, novv not I: but Chriſt liueth in me. And that that I liue novv in the fleſh, I liue in the faith of the ſonne of God, who loued me, & deliuered him ſelf for me.
\v 21 I caſt not avvay the grace of God. For if iuſtice be by the Lavv, then Chriſt died in vaine.
\c 3
\cd By their owne conuerſion at the firſt, 6 and by the example of Abraham, and promiſe made to him, he ſheweth that the vvay to obtaine the benediction, is to ſeeke vnto God by faith in Chriſt. 10 Seing alſo that the Lavv curſeth euery one that hath not euermore kept the Law. 15 And, that the Law was not giuen to alter Gods teſtament, 19 but to conuince the Iewes of ſinne, 23 and ſo to be their pædagogue or leader vnto Chriſt, 25 and then to ceaſe.
\p
\v 1 O Senſles Galatians, vvho hath \f 1 \fr 3.1 \ft For any people or perſon to forſake the faith of their firſt Apoſtles & Conuerſion, at the voice of a few nouellaries, ſeemeth to wiſe men a very bewitching & ſenſeles brutiſhnes. Such is the caſe of our poore countrie, Germanie, and others.\f* bevvitched you, not to obey the truth, before vvhoſe eies \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt vvas proſcribed, being crucified among you?
\v 2 This only I vvould learne of you, By the vvorkes of the Lavv, did you receiue the Spirit, or by the hearing of the faith?
\v 3 Are you ſo fooliſh, that vvhereas you began vvith the ſpirit, now you vvil be conſummate vvith the fleſh?
\v 4 Haue you ſuffered ſo great things vvithout cauſe? if yet vvithout cauſe.
\v 5 He therfore that giueth you the Spirit, and vvorketh miracles among you: by the vvorkes of the Lavv, or by the hearing of the faith doeth he it?
\v 6 As, \x - \xt Gen. 15, 6. Ro. 4, 3.\x* \it Abraham beleeued God, and it vvas reputed to him vnto iuſtice\it*.
\v 7 Know ye therfore that they that are \f 1 \fr 3.7 \ft This faith whereby Abraham vvas iuſtified, and his children the Gentils beleeuing in Chriſt, implieth al Chriſtian vertues, of which the firſt is faith, the ground & foundation of al the reſt, and therfore here and els where often named of the Apoſtle.\f* of faith, the ſame are the children of Abraham.
\v 8 And the Scripture foreſeing that God iuſtifieth the Gentils by faith, ſhewed vnto Abraham before, \x - \xt Gen. 12, 3.\x* \it That in thee ſhal al nations be bleſſed\it*.
\v 9 Therfore they that are of faith, ſhal be bleſſed vvith the faithful Abraham.
\v 10 For vvhoſoeuer are of the vvorkes of the Lavv, are vnder curſe. For it is vvritten: Curſed be euery one that abideth not in al things that be written in the booke of the Lavv, to doe them. \ef - \fr 3.10 \fq Curſed be. \ft ¹ By this place the Heretikes vvould proue that no man is iuſt truely before God, al being guiltie of damnation and Gods curſe, becauſe they keepe not euery iote of the Law. Where in deede the Apoſtle meaneth not ſuch as offend venially (as it is plaine by the place of Deuteronomie whence he reciteth this text) but onely ſuch as commit great and damnable crimes, and ſo by greuous and mortal tranſgreſſions vvholy breake Gods precepts, and thereby incurre the curſe of the Law, from which the ſaid Law could not deliuer them of it ſelf, nor by any other meanes, but by the faith and grace of \+sc CHRIST IESVS\+sc*. \fq ¹ \ft Notwithstanding venial sinnes, men are truely iust, and may keepe the comaundements.\ef*
\v 11 But that in the Law no man is iuſtified vvith God, it is manifeſt, becauſe \x - \xt Abac. 2. Ro. 1.\x* \it The iuſt liueth by faith\it*. \ef - \fr 3.11 \fq Liueth by faith. \ft ¹ It is neither the Heretikes ſpecial preſumption and confidence, nor the faith of Diuels, nor faith vvithout vvorkes which is dead in it ſelf as S. Iames ſaith, ²that can giue life to the iuſt. for that which is dead, can not be the cauſe of life. but it is the Catholike faith, as S. Auguſtine vvriteth, vvhich vvorketh by charitie (according to the Apoſtles ovvne explication of this vvhole paſſage) by vvhich the iuſt liueth. \+it Li. 3 c. 5. cont. duas ep. Pelag\+it*. See the Annotation vpon the ſame wordes. \+it Rom. 1\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft Not only faith. \fq ² \ft \+it Iac. 2.\+it*\ef*
\v 12 But the Lavv is not by faith: but, \x - \xt Lev. 18.\x* \it He that doeth thoſe things, ſhal liue in them\it*.
\v 13 Chriſt hath redeemed vs from the curſe of the Lavv, being made a curſe for vs (becauſe it is vvritten, \x - \xt Deu. 21.\x* \it Curſed is euery one that hangeth on a tree\it*)
\v 14 that on the Gentiles the bleſſing of Abraham might be made in Chriſt \sc IESVS\sc*: that vve may receiue the promiſſe of the Spirit by faith.
\v 15 \f 1 \fr 3.15 \ft The Epiſtle vpon the 13 Sunday after Pentecoſt.\f* Brethren (I ſpeake according to man) yet a mans teſtament being confirmed no man deſpiſeth, or further diſpoſeth.
\v 16 To Abraham vvere the promiſes ſaid, and to his ſeede. He ſaith not, \it And to ſeedes\it*, as in many: but as in one, \x - \xt Gen. 22, 18.\x* \it And to thy ſeede\it*, vvhich is Chriſt.
\v 17 And this I ſay, the teſtament being confirmed of God, the Lavv vvhich vvas made after foure hundred and thirtie yeres, maketh not void to fruſtrate the promiſe.
\v 18 For if the inheritance be of the Lavv, novv not of promiſe. But God gaue it to Abraham by promiſe.
\v 19 VVhy vvas the Lavv then? It vvas put for tranſgreſſions, vntil the ſeede came to vvhom he had promiſed: ordeined by Angels in the hand of a mediatour.
\v 20 And a mediatour is not of one: but God is one.
\v 21 VVas the Lavv then againſt the promiſes of God? God forbid. For if there had been a Lavv giuen that could iuſtifie, vndoubtedly iuſtice ſhould be of the Lavv.
\v 22 But the Scripture \x - \xt Ro. 3, 9. 11, 32.\x* hath concluded al things vnder ſinne: that the promiſe by the faith of \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt might be giuen to them that beleeue. ⊢
\v 23 But before the faith came, vnder the Lavv we vvere kept ſhut vp, vnto that faith which vvas to be reuealed.
\v 24 Therfore the Lavv vvas our Pedagogue in Chriſt: that vve may be iuſtified by faith.
\v 25 But when the faith came, novv vve are not vnder a pædagogue.
\v 26 For you are al the children of God by faith in Chriſt \sc IESVS\sc*.
\v 27 For as many of you as are baptized in Chriſt, haue put on Chriſt. \ef - \fr 3.27 \fq Haue put on Chriſt. \ft ¹ Here the Aduerſaries might haue ſeen, if they vvere not blinded by contentious ſtriuing againſt Gods Church, that when Iuſtification is attributed to faith vvithout mention of good workes or other Chriſtian vertues & Sacraments, it is not meant to exclude any of the ſame from the working of iuſtice or ſaluation. for here vve learne that by the Sacrament of Baptiſme alſo vve put on Chriſt, which is to put on faith, hope, charitie, and al Chriſtian iuſtice. By the ſame vve proue alſo that the Sacraments of the nevv law giue grace, for that the receiuers thereof put on Chriſt. And the Aduerſaries euaſion, that it is faith vvhich worketh in the Sacrament, and not the Sacrament it ſelf, is plainely falſe: Baptiſme giuing grace and faith it ſelf to the infant that had none before. \fq ¹ \ft Baptisme giueth grace and iustification, not faith only.\ef*
\v 28 There is not Ievve nor Greeke, there is not bond nor free, there is not male nor femal. For al you are one in Chriſt \sc IESVS\sc*.
\v 29 And if you be Chriſts, then are you the ſeede of Abraham, heires according to promiſe.
\c 4
\cd That the Law vvas fit for the time of nonnage: but being now come to ful age, to deſire ſuch ſeruitude is abſurd, ſpecially for Gentils. 12 And that he writeth this not of any diſpleaſure, but to tel them the truth, remembring how paſsingly they honoured him when he vvas preſent, and exhorting them therfore not to harken to the falſe Apoſtles in his abſence. 21 By the allegorie alſo of Abrahams two ſonnes, ſhewing, that the children of the Iewes Synagogue ſhal not inherite, but we vvho are the children of the free woman: that is, of the Cath. Church of Chriſt.
\p
\v 1 \sc AND\sc* \f 1 \fr 4.1 \ft The Epiſtle vpon Twelfth eue.\f* I ſay, as long as the heire is a litle one, he differeth nothing from a ſeruant, although he be lord of al,
\v 2 but is vnder tutors and gouernours vntil the time limited of the father:
\v 3 ſo vve alſo, vvhen vve vvere litle ones, vvere ſeruing vnder the \f 1 \fr 4.3 \ft That is, the rudiments of religió, wherin the carnal Iewes were trained vp: or the corporal creatures, wherein their manifold ſacrifices, ſacraments, and rites did conſiſt.\f* elemétes of the vvorld. \ef - \fr 4.3 \fq Seruing. \ft ¹ There can be no external vvorſhip of God nor aſſociation of men in religion, either true or falſe, vvithout the vſe of corporal things or elements. The Heathen ſo vſed the creatures of elements that they ſerued them as their goddes. The Iewes, of vvhom the Apoſtle here ſpeaketh, ſerued not the creatures them ſelues vvhich they occupied in their ceremonies, but they ſerued the only true God vnder the elements: that is to ſay, being ſeruilely clogged, yoked, kept occupied and in awe, vvith innumerable fleſhly, groſſe, and comberſom offices about creatures. The Chriſtians neither ſerue elements, as the one, nor be kept in ſeruile thraldom thereby, as the other: but occupie only a few exceding eaſie, ſwete, ſeemely, and ſignificant, for an agreable exerciſe both of body and minde. ² Vvhereof S. Auguſtine ſaith thus, \+it li. 3. c. 9\+it* de doct. Chriſt. \+it Some fevv for many, moſt eaſie to be done, moſt honorable for ſignification, and moſt cleane and pure for to be obſerued and kept, hath our Lord him ſelf and the Apoſtolical diſcipline deliuered\+it*. And li. de ver. relig. c. 17. \+it Of the wiſedom of God it ſelf mans nature being taken, whereby we vvere called into libertie, a few Sacraments moſt holſom vvere appointed and inſtituted, which might conteine the ſocietie of Chriſtian people, that is, of the free multitude vnder one God\+it*. And againe, cont. Fauſt. li. 19. c 13. \+it The Sacraments are changed, they are made eaſier, fevver, holſommer, happier\+it*. the ſame he hath in the 118 epiſtle c. 1. and many other places beſides. By vvhich you may ſee, it is not al one to vſe elements, viſible Sacraments or ceremonies, and to ſerue them as the Pagans do, or to ſerue vnder them as the Ievves did, wherewith the Heretikes calumniouſly charge the Chriſtians. ³ And as touching the ſmall number, facilitie, efficacie, and ſignification, vvherein the ſaid holy father putteth the ſpecial difference: who ſeeth not that for ſo many buſie ſacrifices, vve haue but one: for Sacraments wel nere infinite, but ſeuen: al ſo eaſie, ſo ful of grace, ſo ſignificant, as can be poſſible, as of euery one in their ſeueral places is proued? ⁴ Here, let the good Readers take heede of a double deceite vſed by the Aduerſaries about S. Auguſtines places alleaged. firſt, in that they ſay he made but tvvo Sacraments, vvhich is vntrue. for, although treating of the difference betvvene the Ievviſh Sacraments and ours, he namely giueth example in Baptiſme and the Euchariſt (as ſometimes alſo for example he nameth but one,) yet he hath no vvord nor ſigne at al that there ſhould be no moe. but contrariewiſe in the foreſaid epiſtle 118 ⁵ he inſinuateth, that beſides thoſe tvvo, there be other of the ſame ſort in the Scriptures. ⁶ Yea, vvith Water and bread, Which be the elements of the tvvo foreſaid Sacraments, he expreſly nameth oile alſo \+it (li. 2 cont. lit. Petil. c. 104\+it*.) the element or matter of the Sacrament of Confirmation: Which in the ſame place he maketh to be a Sacrament as Baptiſme is. So doth he affirme of the Sacrament of Orders \+it li. 1 de bapt. c 1\+it*. and alſo of Matrimonie \+it li. de bono coniug. c. 24\+it*. of Penance likevviſe, he ſpeaketh as of Baptiſme, Which he calleth Reconciliation, \+it li. 1 de adult. coniug. c. 28\+it*. Laſtly, by the booke \+it de viſitatione infirmorum\+it* in S. Auguſtine, \+it li. 2 c. 4\+it*. by Proſper \+it de prædictionibus p. 2. c. 29\+it*. S. Innocentius ad Eugubinum \+it to. 1. Conc. ep. ad Eugub. c. 8\+it*. S. Cyril \+it li. 2 in Leuiticum\+it*, and S. Chryſoſtom \+it li. 3 de Sacerdotio\+it*, ⁷ \+it Extreme vnction\+it* is proued to be a Sacrament. It is falſe then that the Heretikes affirme of S. Auguſtine, by whoſe doctrine it is plaine, that though the elements or Sacraments of the new lavv be but few and very fevv in compariſon of thoſe in the old law, yet there be no fewer then ſeuen ſpecified by him. Vvhich number of ſeuen the holy Councels of Florence and Trent do expreſly define to haue been inſtituted by Chriſt, againſt theſe late Heretikes. See more of theſe Sacraments in their places. Act. 8. 1 Tim. 4. Io. 20. Ia. 5. \+it Epheſ. 5\+it*. ⁸ The other forgerie of the Aduerſaries concerning the elements or ceremonies, is, that S. Auguſtine (ep. 119. c. 19.) ſhould affirme, that the Church and Chriſtian people in his daies (wherevpon they inferre that it is ſo much more novv) were ſo loden with obſeruation of vnprofitable ceremonies, that they were in as great ſeruilitie and ſubiection to ſuch things as the Iewes. He ſaith ſo in deede of ſome particular preſumptions, inuentions, and vſages of certaine perſons, as that ſome made it a heinous matter to touch the ground with their bare feete within their owne octaues, and ſuch like vanities. Whereby ſome ſimple folkes might be infected, vvhich this holy Doctor ſpecially miſliked, and wiſheth ſuch things (as they may, vvithout ſcandal) to be taken avvay. But that he wrote or meant ſo of any ceremonie that the Church vſeth, either appointed by Scripture, or Councel, or cuſtom of the Catholike Church, him ſelf denieth it in expreſſe termes in the ſame place, and in ſundrie other: where he alloweth al the holy ceremonies done in the miniſtration of the Sacraments and els vvhere. Vvhereby it is cleere, that the Churches moſt comely orders and ſignificant rites pertaine not to the yoke of the old law, much leſſe to the ſuperſtition of Gentilitie, as Heretikes affirme: but to the ſweete yoke of Chriſt and light burden of his law, to order, decencie, and inſtruction of the faithful, in al libertie, loue, faith, grace, and ſpirit. \fq ¹ \ft External worship of God by vse of creatures, necessarie: & how the Heathen, Iewes, & Christians differ in the same. \fq ² \ft The vse of external elements in the Sacraments. \fq ³ \ft Our Sacraments few & easie in respect of the Iewes. \fq ⁴ \ft S. Augustine falsely alleaged of the Heretikes for two Sacraments only. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it ep. 118. c. 1. & in ps. 103. conc. 1\+it*. \fq ⁶ \ft The other Sacraments proued out of S. Augustine. \fq ⁷ \ft * \+it Aug. ser. 215 de temp. & de rectitud. Cath. conuersat\+it*. \fq ⁸ \ft S. Augustine falsely alleaged against the ceremonies of the Church.\ef*
\v 4 But vvhen the fulnes of time came, God ſent his ſonne made of a vvoman, made vnder the Lavv:
\v 5 that he might redeeme them that vvere vnder the Lavv, that vve might receiue the adoption of ſonnes.
\v 6 And becauſe you are ſonnes, \x - \xt Ro. 8, 15.\x* God hath ſent the Spirit of his ſonne into your hartes crying: Abba, Father.
\v 7 Therfore now he is not a ſeruant, but a ſonne. And if a ſonne, an heire alſo by God. ⊢
\v 8 But then in deede not knowing God, you ſerued them that by nature are not Gods.
\v 9 But novv vvhen you haue knovven God, or rather are knovven of God: hovv turne you againe to the vveake & poore elements, vvhich you vvil ſerue againe? \ef - \fr 4.9 \fq Weake and poore. \ft Vvhether he meane of the creatures which the Gentils ſerued (as it may ſeeme by the vvordes before of ſeruing ſtrange gods) ſo the elements were moſt baſe and beggerly: or of the Iudaical ceremonies and ſacraments (as moſt expound it) euen ſo alſo their elements vvere vveake and poore in them ſelues, not giuing life, ſaluation, and remiſsion of ſinnes, nor being inſtruments or veſſels of grace, as the 7 Sacraments of the new lavv be.\ef*
\v 10 You obſerue daies, and moneths, and times, and yeres. \ef - \fr 4.10 \fq You obſerue daies. \ft ¹ That which S. Paul ſpeaketh againſt the Idololatrical obſeruation of daies, moneths, and times, dedicated by the Heathen to their falſe goddes, and to wicked men or ſpirites, as to Iupiter, Mercurie, Ianus, Iuno, Diana, and ſuch like, or againſt the ſuperſtitious differences of daies, fatall, fortunate, or diſmol, and other obſeruations of times for good lucke or il lucke in mans actions, gathered either by particular fanſie, or popular obſeruation, or curious and vnlawful artes, or (laſtly) of the Iudaical feſtiuities that vvere then ended and abrogated, vnto which notvvithſtanding certaine Chriſtian Iewes would haue reduced the Galatians againſt the Apoſtles doctrine: al that (I ſay) do the Heretikes of our time falſely and deceitfully interprete againſt the Chriſtian holidaies, and the ſanctification and neceſſarie keeping of the ſame. Which is not only contrarie to the Fathers expoſition, but againſt the very Scriptures, and the practiſe of the Apoſtles & the vvhole Church. \+it Aug. cont. Adimant. c. 16. Ep. 118. c. 7. Hiero. in hunc locum\+it*. In the Apocalypſe c. 1. there is plaine mention of ² the Sunday, that is, our Lordes day \+it (Dominicus dies\+it*) vnto which the Iewes Sabboth vvas altered, their Paſche into our Eaſter, their Pentecoſt into our Whitſontide: vvhich were ordained & obſerued of the Apoſtles them ſelues. And the antiquitie of ³ the feaſtes of Chriſtes Natiuitie, Epiphanie, & Aſcenſion is ſuch, that they can be referred to no other origine but the Apoſtles inſtitution: ⁴vvho (as S. Clement teſtifieth \+it li. 8. conſt. Apoſt. c. 39\+it*.) gaue order for celebrating their fellow Apoſtles, S. Steuens, and other Martyrs daies after their death: and much more no doubt did they giue order for Chriſtes feſtiuities. According to which, the Church hath kept not only his, ⁵ but S. Steuens and the B. Innocents, euen on the ſame daies they be now ſolemnely kept, & his B. mothers, and other Saincts, (as the Aduerſaries them ſelues confeſſe) aboue 1300 yeres, as appeareth in the barbarous combattes betvvene Vveſtphalus the Lutheran, & Caluin, and by the writings betvvixt the Puritans and Proteſtants. For vvhich purpoſe, ſee alſo hovv old the holiday of S. Polycarpe is \+it in Euſebius li. 4 c. 14\+it*: of the ⁶ ⁷ Aſſumption of our Ladie or her dormition in S. Athanaſius, S. Auguſtine, S. Hierom. S. Damaſc. & both of that feaſt and of her Natiuitie in S. Bernard, vvho profeſſeth \+it he receiued them of the Church, & that they ought to be moſt ſolemnely kept\+it*. ep. 174. Wherein vve can not but wonder at the new Church of England, that (though againſt the pure Caluiniſtes wil and doctrine) keepe other Saincts and Apoſtles daies of their death, and yet haue aboliſhed this ſpecial feaſt of our Ladies departure, which they might keepe, though they beleeued not her Aſſumption in body (vvhereof yet S. Denys ⁸ giueth ſo great teſtimonie) being aſſured ſhe is departed at the leaſt: except they either hate her, or thinke her vvorthy of leſſe remembrance then any other Sainct, ⁹ her ſelf prophecying the contrarie of al Catholike generations, that they ſhould bleſſe her. And in deede the Aſſumption is her proper day, as alſo the feaſt of her Natiuitie: the other of the Purification and the Annunciation, which they keepe in England, being not ſo peculiar to her, but belonging rather to Chriſtes Preſentation in the Temple, and his Conception. To conclude, vve may ſee in S. Cyprian \+it ep. 34\+it*. Origen \+it ho. 3. in diuers\+it*. Tertullian \+it de cor. mil\+it*. ¹⁰ S. Gregorie Nazianzene \+it de amore pauperum\+it*. the Councel of Gangres, ¹¹yea and in the councel of Nice it ſelf giuing order for Eaſter and the certaine celebrating thereof, that Chriſtian Feſtiuities be holy, auncient, and to be obſerued on preſcript daies and times, and that this is not Iudaical obſeruation of daies, as Aërius taught, for which he was condemned of Hereſie, as S. Epiphanius ¹² witneſſeth. But of holidaies S. Auguſtine ſheweth both the reaſon and his liking, in theſe memorable wordes. Firſt for the feaſtes belonging to our Lord, thus: ¹³ ¹⁴ \+it We dedicate and conſecrate the memorie of Gods benefites with ſolemnities, feaſtes, and certaine appointed daies, leſt by tract of times there might creepe in ingrateful and vnkinde obliuion\+it*. Of the feſtiuities of Martyrs thus: \+it Chriſtian people celebrate the memories of Martyrs with religious ſolemnitie, both to moue them ſelues to imitation of them, and that they may be partakers of their merites, and be holpen with their praiers. Cont. Fauſt. li. 20 c. 21\+it*. And of al Saincts daies, thus: \+it Keepe ye and celebrate with ſobrietie the Natiuities of Saincts, that we may imitate them which haue gone before Vs, and they may reioyce of vs which pray for vs. In ps. 88. Conc. 2. in fine\+it*. ¹⁵ And as is ſaid of preſcript daies of feaſtes, ſo the like is to be ſaid ¹⁶ of faſtes, which els vvhere vve haue ſhewed to be of the Apoſtles ordinance. And ſo alſo of the Eccleſiaſtical diuiſion of the yere into Aduent, Septuageſme, &c. the vveeke into ſo many Feries, ¹⁷ the day into Houres of praiers, as the Prime, the Third, the Sixth, the None &c. Vvhereof ſee ¹⁸ S. Cyprian, vvho deriueth theſe things by the Scriptures from the Apoſtles alſo, and counteth theſe things vvhich the vvicked Heretikes reproue, to be ful of myſterie. Like vnto this alſo is it, ¹⁹ that the holy Scriptures were ſo diſpoſed of, and deuided, that certaine peeces (as is alwaies obſerued and practiſed vntil this day) ſhould be read at one time, and others at other times and ſeaſons, through out the yere, according to the diuerſitie of our Lordes actions and benefites, or the Saincts ſtories then recorded. Which the Puritane Caluiniſts alſo condemne of ſuperſtition, deſiring to bring in helliſh horrour and al diſorder. See conc. Carthag. 3. c. 47. & pag. 288 of this booke. \fq ¹ \ft The Heathenish and Iudaical obseruation of daies heretically compared with the Christian obseruation of festiuities and holy daies &c. \fq ² \ft Sunday, Easter, whitsontide. \fq ³ \ft The festiuities of Christ. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Aug. ep. 28. et Ser. de Sanctis. Fulgent. Leo\+it*. \fq ⁵ \ft Other holidaies of Saincts. \fq ⁶ \ft Festiuities of our B. Ladie. \fq ⁷ \ft * \+it See the Annotation, Act. 1. v. 14\+it*. \fq ⁸ \ft \+it ep. ad Timotheum\+it*. \fq ⁹ \ft \+it Luc. 1. v. 48\+it*. \fq ¹⁰ \ft See S. Grego. li. \+it 7\+it*. ep. \+it 29\+it*. of Martyrs feasts al the yere, and Masses in the same. \fq ¹¹ \ft \+it Conc. Gangr. c. 20\+it* \fq ¹² \ft Epiphan. Hær. 75. \fq ¹³ \ft S. Augustine's words of Festivities and holy-days. \fq ¹⁴ \ft \+it Aug. de Ciuit. Dei li. 10. c. 16\+it*. \fq ¹⁵ \ft Prescript fasting daies. \fq ¹⁶ \ft * \+it Hilar. prolog. in psal. explan. Epip. hær. 75. & in fine. li. 3 cont. hær\+it*. \fq ¹⁷ \ft Canonical houres. \fq ¹⁸ \ft * \+it Cypri. de orat. Domi. 15\+it*. \fq ¹⁹ \ft Reading of the Scriptures according to the time of the yere.\ef*
\v 11 I feare you, leſt perhaps I haue laboured in vaine among you.
\v 12 Be ye as I, becauſe I alſo am as you: brethren, I beſeeche you, you haue hurt me nothing.
\v 13 And you knovv that by infirmitie of the fleſh I euangelized to you heretofore:
\v 14 and your tentation in my fleſh you deſpiſed not, neither reiected, but \f 1 \fr 4.14 \ft So ought al Catholike people receiue their teachers in religion, vvith al duetie, loue, & reuerence.\f* as an Angel of God you receiued me, as Chriſt \sc IESVS\sc*.
\v 15 VVhere is then your bleſſednes? for I giue you teſtimonie that if it could be done, you vvould haue plucked out your eies and haue giuen them to me.
\v 16 Am I then become your enemie, telling you the truth?
\v 17 They emulate you not vvel: but they vvould exclude you, that you might emulate them.
\v 18 But do you emulate the good in good alvvaies: and not only vvhen I am preſent vvith you.
\v 19 My litle children, vvhom I trauail vvithal againe, vntil Chriſt be formed in you.
\v 20 And I vvould be vvith you now and chaunge my voice: becauſe I am confounded in you.
\v 21 Tel me you that vvil be vnder the Lavv, haue you not read the Law?
\v 22 \x - \xt Gen. 16, 15. 21, 2.\x* For it is vvritten that \f 2 \fr 4.22 \ft The Epiſtle vpon the 4 Sunday in Lent.\f* Abraham had tvvo ſonnes: one of the bond-vvoman, and one of the free-vvoman.
\v 23 But he that of the bond-vvoman, vvas borne according to the fleſh: and he that of the free-vvoman, by the promiſſe.
\v 24 vvhich things are ſaid by an allegorie. For theſe are the two teſtaments. The one from mount Sina, gendring vnto bondage: vvhich is Agar, \ef - \fr 4.24 \fq By an allegorie. \ft ¹ Here vve learne that the holy Scriptures haue beſide the litteral ſenſe, a deeper ſpiritual and more principal meaning: which is not only to be taken of the holy vvordes, but of the very factes and perſons reported: both the ſpeaches and the actions being ſignificatiue ouer and aboue the letter. Vvhich pregnancie of manifold ſenſes if S. Paul had not ſignified him ſelf in certaine places, the Heretikes had bene leſſe wicked and preſumptuous in condemning the holy fathers allegorical expoſitions almoſt vvholy: who now ſhew them ſelues to be mere brutiſh and carnal men, hauing no ſenſe nor feeling of the profunditie of the Scriptures, vvhich our holy fathers the Doctors of Gods Church ſavv. \fq ¹ \ft The Scriptures haue an allegorical sense beside the literal.\ef*
\v 25 (for Sina is a mountaine in Arabia, \f 1 \fr 4.25 \ft \+it c συστοιχεῖ\+it*\f* vvhich hath affinitie to that vvhich novv is Hieruſalem) and ſerueth vvith her children.
\v 26 But that Hieruſalem vvhich is aboue, is free: vvhich is our mother.
\v 27 For it is vvritten: \x - \xt Eſa. 54, 1.\x* \it Reioyce thou barren, that beareſt not: breake forth and crie, that trauaileſt not: becauſe many are the children of the deſolate, more then of her that hath a huſband\it*.
\v 28 But \x - \xt Ro. 9, 8.\x* we brethren, according to Iſaac, are the children of promis.
\v 29 But \f 1 \fr 4.29 \ft This mutual perſecution, is a figure alſo of the Church iuſtly perſecuting Heretikes, & contrariwiſe of Heretikes (vvhich be the childré of the bondwoman) vniuſtly perſecuting the Catholike Church. \+it Aug. ep. 48\+it*.\f* as then he that vvas borne according to the fleſh, perſecuted him that vvas after the ſpirit: ſo now alſo.
\v 30 But vvhat ſaith the Scripture? \x - \xt Gen. 21, 10.\x* \it Caſt out the bond-woman and her ſonne. for the ſonne of the bond-vvoman ſhal not be heire vvith the ſonne of the free-woman\it*.
\v 31 Therfore brethren we are not the children of the bond-vvoman, but of the free: by the freedom vvherevvith Chriſt hath made vs free. ⊢ \ef - \fr 4.31 \fq Freedom. \ft ¹ He meaneth the libertie and diſcharge from the old ceremonies, ſacraments, and the vvhole bondage of the Law, and from the ſeruitude of ſinne, and the Diuel, to ſuch as obey him: but not libertie to do what euery man liſt, or to be vnder no obedience of ſpiritual or temporal lavves and gouerners: not a licence neuer to pray, faſt, keepe holyday, or vvorkday, but when and how it ſeemeth beſt to euery mans phantaſie. Such a diſſolute licentious ſtate is farre from the true libertie which Chriſt purchaſed for vs. \fq ¹ \ft True Christian libertie.\ef*
\c 5
\cd Againſt the lie of the falſe Apoſtles, he proteſteth his mind of Circumciſion. 13 and teſtifieth, that they are called to libertie. But yet leſt any miſconſter Chriſtian libertie, he telleth them that they ſhal not inherite the kingdom, vnles they abſtaine from the workes of the fleſh, which are al mortal ſinnes: and do the fruitful workes of the Spirit, fulfilling al the commaundements of the Law by Charitie.
\p
\v 1 \sc STAND\sc*, and be not holden in againe vvith the yoke of ſeruitude.
\v 2 Behold I Paul tel you that if you be circumciſed, Chriſt ſhal profite you nothing.
\v 3 And I teſtifie againe to euery man circumciding him ſelf, that he is a detter to doe the vvhole Lavv.
\v 4 You are euacuated from Chriſt, that are iuſtified in the Lavv: you are fallen from grace.
\v 5 For vve in ſpirit, by faith, expect the hope of iuſtice.
\v 6 For in Chriſt \sc IESVS\sc* \x - \xt Gal. 6, 15.\x* neither circumciſion auaileth ought, nor prepuce: but faith that vvorketh by charitie. \ef - \fr 5.6 \fq Faith. \ft This iſ the faith vvorking by charitie, vvhich S. Paul meaneth els vvhere, vvhen he ſaith that faith doth iuſtifie. And note vvel that by theſe termes circumciſion & prepuce not auailable to iuſtification, it is plaine that in other places he meaneth the workes of Circumciſion and Prepuce (that is, of the Iewes and the Gentils) vvithout faith, vvhich auaile not, but faith working by charitie: as vvho ſhould ſay, faith and good workes, not vvorkes without faith. ¹ Againe note here, that if the Proteſtants who pretend conference of places to be the beſt or only vvay to explicate hard ſpeaches of the holy Scriptures, had folowed but their ovvne rule, this one text vvould haue interpreted & cleared vnto them al other vvhereby iuſtice and ſaluation might ſeeme to be attributed to faith alone: the Apoſtle here ſo expreſly ſetting dovvne, the faith which he commendeth ſo much before, not to be alone, but vvith charitie: not to be idle, but to be vvorking by Charitie: as S. Auguſtine noteth. \+it de fid. & op. c. 14\+it*. ² Further the good Reader muſt obſerue, that whereas the Proteſtants ſome of them confeſſe, that Charitie and good workes be ioyned and requiſite alſo, and that they exclude them not, but commend them highly, yet ſo that the ſaid Charitie or good vvorkes are no part of our iuſtice or any cauſe of iuſtification, but as fruites and effectes of faith onely, vvhich they ſay doth all, yea though the other be preſent: this falſe gloſe alſo is reproued euidently by this place, vvhich teacheth vs cleane contrarie: to vvitte, ³ that faith hath her vvhole actiuitie and operation toward iuſtice and ſaluation, of charitie, and not contrarievviſe: vvithout which it can not haue any act meritorious or agreable to God for our ſaluation. for vvhich cauſe S. Auguſtine ſaith, \+it li. 15. de Trin. c. 18. Fidem non facit vtilem niſi charitas, nothing maketh faith profitable but charitie\+it*. But the Heretikes anſwer, that where the Apoſtle ſaith, \+it faith vvorketh by charitie\+it*, he maketh charitie to be the inſtrument only of faith in wel working, and therfore the inferior cauſe at the leaſt. but this alſo is eaſily refuted by the Apoſtles plaine teſtimonie, affirming that charitie is ⁴ the greater vertue, & that if a man had al faith and lacked charitie, he vvere worth nothing. And againe, that Charitie is the ⁵ perfection and ⁶ accópliſhment of the Law (as faith is not) vvhich can not agree to the inſtrumental or inferior cauſe. ⁷ And therfore when it is ſaid that faith vvorketh by charitie, it is not as by an inſtrument, but as the body worketh by the ſoul, the matter by the forme, vvithout which they haue no actiuitie. Vvherevpon the the Schooles call Charitie, the forme or life of faith, that is to ſay, the force, actiuitie, & operatiue qualitie thereof, in reſpect of merite and iuſtice. Vvhich S. Iames doth plainely inſinuate, vvhen he maketh faith without Charitie, to be as a dead corps vvithout ſoul or life, and therfore vvithout profitable operation. c. 2. v. 26. \fq ¹ \ft Iustificatió by faith only, disproued by conference of Scriptures. \fq ² \ft How the Protestants admit charitie and good workes to iustificatió. \fq ³ \ft Charitie is more principal then faith in iustificatió. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Cor. 13\+it*. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Rom. 13\+it*. \fq ⁶ \ft \+it 1 Tim. 1\+it*. \fq ⁷ \ft How faith worketh by charitie.\ef*
\v 7 You ranne vvel, vvho hath hindered you not to obey the truth?
\v 8 The perſuaſion is not of him that calleth you. \x - \xt 1. Cor. 5, 6.\x*
\v 9 A litle leauen corrupteth the vvhole paſte.
\v 10 I haue confidence in you in our Lord: that you vvil be of no other minde: but he that troubleth you, ſhal beare the iudgement, vvhoſoeuer he be.
\v 11 And as for me, brethren, if as yet I preach circumciſion, vvhy doe I yet ſuffer perſecution? then is the ſcandal of the croſſe euacuated.
\v 12 I would they vvere alſo cut of that trouble you.
\v 13 For you, brethren, are called into libertie: only make not this libertie an occaſion to the fleſh, but by charitie ſerue one an other. \ef - \fr 5.13 \fq Libertie an occaſion. \ft ¹ They abuſe the libertie of the Goſpel to the aduantage of their fleſh, that vnder pretenſe thereof, ſhake of their obedience to the lavves of man, to the decrees of the Church and Councels, that vvil liue and beleeue as they liſt, and not be taught by their Superiors, but fornicate with euery Sect-maſter that teacheth pleaſant & licentious things: and al this vnder pretence of ſpirit, libertie, and freedom of the Goſpel. Such muſt learne that al hereſies, ſchiſmes, and rebellions againſt the Church and their lavvful Prelates, be counted here among the workes of the fleſh. See S. Auguſtine \+it de fid. & op. c. 24. 25\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft True libertie, not carnal and fleshly.\ef*
\v 14 \x - \xt Lev. 19, 18.\x* For al the Lavv is fulfilled in one vvord: \f 2 \fr 5.14 \ft The Epiſtle vpon the 14 Sunday after Pentecoſt.\f* \it Thou ſhalt loue thy neighbour as thy ſelf\it*.
\v 15 But if you bite and eate one an other: take heede you be not conſumed one of an other.
\v 16 And I ſay, vvalke in the ſpirit, and the luſtes of the fleſh you ſhal not accompliſh.
\v 17 For the fleſh luſteth againſt the ſpirit: and the ſpirit againſt the fleſh. for theſe are aduerſaries one to an other: \f 1 \fr 5.17 \ft Here men thinke \+it (ſaith S. Auguſtine\+it*) the Apoſtle denieth that we haue free libertie of vvil: not vnderſtanding that this is ſaid to them, if they wil not hold faſt the grace of faith conceiued, by vvhich only they cã walke in the ſpirit, & not accópliſh the concupiſcences of the fleſh. \+it in c. 5. Gal\+it*.\f* that not vvhat things ſoeuer you vvil, theſe you doe.
\v 18 But if you be ledde by the ſpirit, you are not vnder the Lavv.
\v 19 And the workes of the fleſh be manifeſt, vvhich are, fornication, vncleannes, impudicitie, lecherie,
\v 20 ſeruing of Idols, vvitch-craftes, enmities, contentions, emulations, angers, bravvles, diſſenſions, ſectes,
\v 21 enuies, murders, ebrieties, commeſſations, and ſuch like. vvhich I foretel you, as I haue foretold you, that they vvhich doe \f 1 \fr 5.21 \ft c S. Auguſtine ſheweth hereby that not only infidelitie is a damnable ſinne.\f* ſuch things, ſhal not obteine the kingdom of God.
\v 22 But the fruite of the Spirit is, Charitie, ioy, peace, patience, benignitie, goodnes, longanimitie,
\v 23 mildnes, faith, modeſtie, cótinencie, chaſtitie. Againſt ſuch there is no lavv.
\v 24 And they that be Chriſts, haue crucified their fleſh vvith the vices and concupiſcences. ⊢
\v 25 \f 1 \fr 5.25 \ft The Epiſtle vpon the 15 Sunday after Pentecoſt.\f* If vve liue in the ſpirit, in the ſpirit alſo let vs vvalke.
\v 26 Let vs not be made deſirous of vaine glorie, prouoking one an other, enuying one an other.
\c 6
\cd If any do ſinne, the reſt that do the workes of the Holy Ghoſt, muſt not therfore take pride in them ſelues, but rather make humilitie of it, partly by fearing their owne fall, partly by looking ſtraitly to their owne vvorkes. 6 He exhorteth earneſtly to good workes, aſſuring them that they ſhal reape none other then here they ſovv. 11 With his owne hand he writeth, telling them, the true cauſe why thoſe falſe Apoſtles preach circumciſion, to be only to pleaſe the Iewes: 17 and a plaine argument that he preacheth it not, to be this, that he is perſecuted of the Iewes.
\p
\v 1 \sc BRETHREN\sc*, and if a man be preoccupated in any fault, you that are ſpiritual, inſtruct ſuch an one in the ſpirit of lenitie, conſidering thine owne ſelf, leſt thou alſo be tempted.
\v 2 Beare ye one an others burdens: & ſo you ſhal fulfil the lavv of Chriſt.
\v 3 For if any man eſteeme him ſelf to be ſomething, vvhereas he is nothing, he ſeduceth him ſelf.
\v 4 But let euery one proue his ovvne vvorke, & ſo in him ſelf only ſhal he haue the glorie, and not in an other.
\v 5 For euery one ſhal beare his ovvne burden.
\v 6 And let \x - \xt 1. Cor. 9, 7.\x* him that is catechized in the vvord, communicate to him that catechizeth him, in al his goods. \ef - \fr 6.6 \fq Communicate. \ft ¹ The great duety & reſpect that vve ought to haue to ſuch as preach or teach vs the Cath. faith. and not in regard onely of their paines taken with vs, and vvel-deſeruing of vs by their doctrine: but that vve may be partakers of their merites, we ought ſpecially to do good to ſuch, or (as the Apoſtle ſpeaketh) cómunicate with them in al our temporal goods, that vve may be partakers of their ſpiritual. See S. Auguſtine \+it li. 2. Euang. queſt. q. 8\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft Duety to our spiritual teachers.\ef*
\v 7 Be not deceiued, God is not mocked. For what things a man ſhal ſow, thoſe alſo ſhal he reape.
\v 8 For he that ſovveth in his fleſh, of the fleſh alſo ſhal reape corruption. but he that ſoweth in the ſpirit, of the ſpirit ſhal reape life euerlaſting.
\v 9 And \x - \xt 2. Thes. 3, 13.\x* doing good, let vs not faile. For in due time vve ſhal \f 2 \fr 6.9 \ft The workes of mercie be the ſeede of life euerlaſting, and the proper cauſe thereof, and not faith only.\f* reape not failing.
\v 10 Therfore vvhiles vve haue time, let vs vvorke good to al, but eſpecially to the domeſticals of the faith. ⊢ \ef - \fr 6.10 \fq Eſpecially. \ft ¹ In giuing almes, though vve may do wel in helping al that are in neceſſitie, as farre as vve can, yet we are more bound to ſuccour Chriſtians, then Iewes or Infidels: and Catholikes, then Heretikes. See S. Hierom \+it q. 1. ad Hedibiam\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft In almes whom to preferre.\ef*
\v 11 See vvith vvhat maner of letters I haue written to you vvith mine ovvne hand.
\v 12 VVhoſoeuer vvil pleaſe in the fleſh, they force you to be circumciſed, only that they may not ſuffer the perſecution of the croſſe of Chriſt.
\v 13 For neither they that are circumciſed, do keepe the Law: but they vvil haue you to be circumciſed, that they may glorie in your fleſh.
\v 14 \f 1 \fr 6.14 \ft b The Epiſtle for S. Francis, Octob. 4.\f* But \f 2 \fr 6.14 \ft Chriſt \+it (ſaith S. Auguſtine\+it*) choſe a kinde of death, to hang on the Croſſe and to fixe or faſten the ſame croſſe in the foreheads of the faithful, that the Chriſtian may ſay, God forbid that I ſhould glorie ſauing in the croſſe of our Lord IESVS CHRIST. Expos. in Euang. Io. tract. 45.\f* God forbid that I ſhould glorie, ſauing in the croſſe of our Lord \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt: by vvhom the vvorld is crucified to me, and I to the vvorld.
\v 15 For in Chriſt \sc IESVS\sc* neither circumciſion auaileth ought, nor prepuce, but a nevv creature. \ef - \fr 6.15 \fq A new creature. \ft ¹ Note vvel that the Apoſtle calleth that here a new creature, vvhich in the laſt chapter he termed, \+it faith working by charitie\+it*, & (1 Cor. 7, 19) \+it the obſeruatió of the cómaundeméts of God\+it*. Vvhereby vve may learne that vnder the name of faith is conteined the whole reformation of our ſoules and our new creation in good workes. and alſo that Chriſtian iuſtice is a very qualitie, condition and ſtate of vertue and grace reſident in vs, and not a phantaſtical apprehenſion of Chriſtes iuſtice only imputed to vs. ² Laſtly, that the faith which iuſtifieth, ioyned with the other vertues, is properly the formal cauſe, and not the efficient or inſtrumental cauſe of iuſtification. that is to ſay, theſe vertues put together, being the effect of Gods grace, be our new creature and our iuſtice in Chriſt. \fq ¹ \ft Iustice an inherent qualitie in vs. \fq ² \ft Faith with the other vertues is the formal cause of iustification.\ef*
\v 16 And vvhoſoeuer ſhal folovv this rule, peace vpon them, and mercie, and vpon the Iſraël of God.
\v 17 From hencefurth let no man be troubleſome to me. for I beare the markes of our Lord \sc IESVS\sc* in my body.
\v 18 The grace of our Lord \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt be vvith your ſpirit brethren. Amen. ⊢
